      ":question/id": "8f7dc381-ff4a-5ab2-9c80-1b180b43d77b",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-2252",
      ":question/problem": "Find the antiderivative of $2{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    "key_origin": "missing fields; unsupported"
  },
